    "prompt": "You are extracting the numeric claims of one section of a model-validation report, so that each\ncan be checked against the artifact it cites. Section: outcomes.\n\nThe prose is printed below one line per line, each prefixed with its own number and a vertical\nbar. List EVERY number that appears in the prose, in the order it appears, including numbers that\nappear inside a citation's logical name and numbers written with a thousands separator or a per\ncent sign. For each one:\n\n- \"line\" is the number printed to the left of the line the number appears on. Give the number\n  only; do not copy the line's text back, and do not count the prefix itself as a number.\n- \"value\" is the number as written, with thousands separators and the per cent sign removed:\n  \"3,500\" is 3500, \"22.0%\" is 22.0.\n- \"unit\" is \"percent\" when the number is written with a per cent sign, \"count\" for a number of\n  rows, features or events, \"currency\", \"bp\" for basis points, \"months\" for a horizon, and\n  \"ratio\" for every other dimensionless quantity (AUC, PSI, VIF, a coefficient, a lift).\n- \"metric\" is what the number measures, taken from the logical name of the artifact it cites\n  (auc, brier, psi, vif, calibration_slope, delta_auc, coefficient, event_rate, ...), or null.\n- \"split\" is train, test, out_of_time or vintage_holdout, or null.\n- \"comparison\" is \"eq\" for a number that equals its artifact; \"delta\" for a difference written\n  \"from a to b\" or \"a gap of x\", whose value is the second cited artifact minus the first; and\n  \"ratio\" for a number written as one artifact divided by another. A delta or ratio carries TWO\n  adjacent citations, in that order.\n- \"citation\" is the [[art:...]] token the number carries, copied exactly, or null when the\n  sentence carries none. Do not invent a citation and do not move one from another number.\n- \"rounding\" is the number of decimals the prose wrote when it wrote fewer than the artifact has\n  (2 for a lift written 2.24), or null.\n\nReturn every number even when it has no citation: a number you leave out is still counted, and\ncounted against the report.\n\nProse:\n 1| | Split | n | loans | events (approx.) | AUC | KS | slope | CPR act. | CPR pred. | error |\n 2| |---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n 3| | train | 36,013 | 934 | 307 | 0.7883 | 0.456 | 0.994 | 9.76% | 9.66% | −1.1% |\n 4| | test | 15,382 | 400 | 124 | 0.7753 | 0.416 | 0.937 | 9.26% | 9.32% | +0.6% |\n 5| | out_of_time | 12,257 | 515 | 220 | 0.7846 | 0.432 | 0.997 | 19.53% | 20.43% | +4.6% |\n 6| | vintage_holdout | 32,177 | 666 | 155 | 0.7718 | 0.452 | 0.837 | 5.63% | 6.57% | **+16.7%** |\n 7| \n 8| **Discrimination is stable and there is no out-of-sample degradation.** The train-to-test AUC gap is 0.013, train-to-vintage 0.017, and train-to-out-of-time only 0.003. With roughly 124 events in the test split, the standard error on its AUC is on the order of 0.03, so none of these gaps is distinguishable from sampling noise. KS tracks AUC consistently across all four splits. I record no O1 defect. That the model holds 0.785 AUC through a regime in which the event rate doubled is a genuine strength and should be stated as such: the rate-response covariates are doing real work, not merely fitting the training period's level.\n 9| \n10| **Calibration in the training period and out of time is good.** Slopes of 0.994 and 0.997, with mean predicted within 1% and 5% of observed respectively. The out-of-time result is the more impressive of the two — the model absorbed a doubling of the prepayment rate and still landed within 90 basis points of CPR (20.4% predicted against 19.5% actual), with the modest error in the conservative direction for an MSR holder only in the sense of being pessimistic on value.\n11| \n12| **Calibration on the vintage holdout fails.** The slope is 0.837, outside any conventional [0.90, 1.10] acceptance band and the worst of the four splits by a wide margin. More consequentially, the level is biased: mean predicted 0.005647 against an observed event rate of 0.004817, a 17.2% relative over-prediction of the monthly hazard, which annualises to 6.57% predicted CPR against 5.63% actual. For a prepayment model whose output feeds MSR valuation, a systematic 17% overstatement of CPR on a whole vintage cohort translates more or less directly into a systematic understatement of asset value on that cohort. This is the report's principal finding.\n13| \n14| The pattern across splits is informative about cause. Error is small where the observed prepayment rate is near the training level (test, +0.6%), moderate where it is far above (out-of-time, +4.6%), and large where it is far below (vintage holdout, +16.7%). The vintage holdout is also the most seasoned population — 32,177 rows over 666 loans, 48 months per loan, against 39 for train — and these are loans that persisted without prepaying. The model's baseline hazard peaks at age 43 and decays only gently thereafter ([[art:b764932a:run.model_summary#baseline_hazard]]). A slow-burning, seasoned, low-propensity cohort is precisely where too shallow a post-peak decay, or too weak a burnout effect, would show up as over-prediction. The slope below 1.0 says the same thing from the other side: the model's high scores are too high. I regard under-modelled burnout and an over-flat post-peak hazard as the leading hypothesis, and it is directly testable once the coefficient vector is disclosed.\n15| \n16| The test slope of 0.937 is within band but sits toward its lower edge and points the same way. I treat it as corroborating rather than as a separate defect.\n17| \n18| **No challenger.** The artifact store contains exactly one model. There is no benchmark — not a logistic regression on the same design matrix, not a gradient-boosted alternative, not an industry S-curve — against which the 0.775 test AUC can be judged. An AUC of 0.775 on monthly prepayment is a plausible-looking number, and that is the problem: without a challenger there is no way to know whether the spline-plus-covariate structure is earning its complexity or whether a simpler specification would match it. Effective challenge is unmet.\n\n\nAnswer with one JSON object and nothing else: no prose before or after it, no explanation, no markdown fence. It must validate against this JSON schema:\n{\"$defs\": {\"Comparison\": {\"description\": \"What the claimed value is asserted to equal.\\n\\n``delta`` and ``ratio`` are cited by two adjacent ``[[art:...]]`` tokens, in order (D-012).\\nThe enum is not nullable: ``eq`` is the explicit default, so \\\"this number equals its artifact\\\"\\nhas one spelling and not two (D-018).\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    eq: The value matches the cited artifact.\\n    delta: The value matches ``second - first``.\\n    ratio: The value matches ``second / first``.\", \"enum\": [\"eq\", \"delta\", \"ratio\"], \"title\": \"Comparison\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"ExtractedClaim\": {\"additionalProperties\": false, \"description\": \"One claim as the model returns it: the grammar minus the fields the caller already knows.\\n\\n``text``, ``section``, ``id`` and ``source`` are not asked for. The caller knows which section\\nit passed in, the id is a hash of the other three fields, the source of an extraction is\\nalways ``report``, and the text is the line the model named -- which the caller has in front\\nof it, so asking for it back costs output tokens and adds a way for the answer to disagree\\nwith the prose (D-063, D-085).\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    line: The 1-based number of the line the number appears on, as the prompt printed it.\\n    value: The number as written, separators and per cent sign removed.\\n    unit: How it is written.\\n    metric: What it measures, or ``None``.\\n    split: Which split it is about, or ``None``.\\n    comparison: ``eq``, ``delta`` or ``ratio``.\\n    citation: The citation the number carries, or ``None``.\\n    rounding: Decimals the prose used, or ``None``.\", \"properties\": {\"line\": {\"minimum\": 1, \"title\": \"Line\", \"type\": \"integer\"}, \"value\": {\"title\": \"Value\", \"type\": \"number\"}, \"unit\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/Unit\", \"default\": \"ratio\"}, \"metric\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Metric\"}, \"split\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/SplitName\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null}, \"comparison\": {\"$ref\": \"#/$defs/Comparison\", \"default\": \"eq\"}, \"citation\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"type\": \"string\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Citation\"}, \"rounding\": {\"anyOf\": [{\"minimum\": 0, \"type\": \"integer\"}, {\"type\": \"null\"}], \"default\": null, \"title\": \"Rounding\"}}, \"required\": [\"line\", \"value\"], \"title\": \"ExtractedClaim\", \"type\": \"object\"}, \"SplitName\": {\"description\": \"The splits a claim may be stated on, exactly as ``CLAIMS_SCHEMA.json`` enumerates them.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    train: The development split.\\n    test: The held-out split.\\n    out_of_time: A later-period split; hazard subjects.\\n    vintage_holdout: One origination year held out; hazard subjects.\", \"enum\": [\"train\", \"test\", \"out_of_time\", \"vintage_holdout\"], \"title\": \"SplitName\", \"type\": \"string\"}, \"Unit\": {\"description\": \"How a number is written, which decides how it is normalised before matching.\\n\\nAttributes:\\n    ratio: Every dimensionless quantity not written with a ``%`` -- AUC, PSI, VIF, a\\n        coefficient, a lift.\\n    percent: Written with a ``%``; normalised against a unit-interval artifact by dividing\\n        by 100.\\n    count: A number of rows, features or events. Matched to half a unit, so integers match\\n        exactly.\\n    currency: A money amount.\\n    bp: Basis points, as a rate shock is written.\\n    months: A horizon.\", \"enum\": [\"ratio\", \"percent\", \"count\", \"currency\", \"bp\", \"months\"], \"title\": \"